1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,000
[MUSIC]

2
00:00:15,940 --> 00:00:19,717
In our last lesson we talked about
the four fundamental components of

3
00:00:19,717 --> 00:00:21,610
Android applications.

4
00:00:21,610 --> 00:00:27,910
And they were activities, services,
broadcast receivers and content providers.

5
00:00:29,900 --> 00:00:33,470
Today we're going to take a deeper
look at one of those components,

6
00:00:33,470 --> 00:00:34,650
the activity class.

7
00:00:35,700 --> 00:00:39,060
I'll start by presenting
the activity class itself.

8
00:00:39,060 --> 00:00:43,050
Next I'll discuss Androids Task Backstack
which helps you to

9
00:00:43,050 --> 00:00:47,900
easily navigate back and
forth among the activities they use.

10
00:00:47,900 --> 00:00:51,450
After that,
I'll discuss the lifecycle of activities.

11
00:00:51,450 --> 00:00:56,280
How they're created, executed, and
terminated, and how Android manages and

12
00:00:56,280 --> 00:01:01,880
communicates these lifecycle phases and
changes to your applications.

13
00:01:01,880 --> 00:01:03,980
After that, I'll discuss the APIs and

14
00:01:03,980 --> 00:01:08,380
patterns that you'll need to
programmatically start activities.

15
00:01:08,380 --> 00:01:11,320
And finally, I'll finish up with a,
with a discussion of

16
00:01:11,320 --> 00:01:16,390
how Android activities handle device and
application configuration changes.

17
00:01:16,390 --> 00:01:23,360
As I said before, activities are the
primary class for interacting with users.

18
00:01:23,360 --> 00:01:27,120
They're designed to provide a visual
interface through which the user can

19
00:01:27,120 --> 00:01:28,460
interact with the application.

20
00:01:28,460 --> 00:01:34,460
And by convention, activities should be
modular, in the sense that, each activity

21
00:01:34,460 --> 00:01:39,660
should support a single focused thing that
the user can do with your application.

22
00:01:39,660 --> 00:01:44,080
And individual things like viewing an
email message or showing a log in screen.

23
00:01:45,210 --> 00:01:49,120
And if you follow this convention,
then you end up creating applications by

24
00:01:49,120 --> 00:01:53,880
stringing together multiple activities,
each with a single purpose.

25
00:01:53,880 --> 00:01:56,790
That the user then navigates through, and

26
00:01:56,790 --> 00:02:01,240
Android helps users do this
navigation in a couple of ways.

27
00:02:01,240 --> 00:02:06,030
By supporting the concept of tasks,
by providing a task back stack for

28
00:02:06,030 --> 00:02:11,070
managing the specific activities through
which the user is navigating, and

29
00:02:11,070 --> 00:02:14,020
by ensuring that activities
are properly suspended and

30
00:02:14,020 --> 00:02:18,340
resumed as they're pushed on and
popped off the task back stack.

31
00:02:19,690 --> 00:02:23,740
In Android, a task is simply
a set of related activities.

32
00:02:23,740 --> 00:02:28,610
These related activities can, but don't
have to be part of the same application.

33
00:02:28,610 --> 00:02:34,290
So tasks can span multiple applications,
and most tasks start at the home screen.

34
00:02:34,290 --> 00:02:37,280
So when a user launches
an application from the home screen,

35
00:02:37,280 --> 00:02:39,820
a new task is normally started.

36
00:02:39,820 --> 00:02:43,240
And when the user hits the home button,
to return back to the home screen.

37
00:02:43,240 --> 00:02:47,259
The current task is it,
is at least temporarily closed.

38
00:02:49,450 --> 00:02:52,320
The task backstack works as follows.

39
00:02:52,320 --> 00:02:53,390
When an activity is launched,

40
00:02:53,390 --> 00:02:57,790
it's pushed on to the task backstack,
normally as part of the current task.

41
00:02:58,840 --> 00:03:02,710
When that activity is later destroyed,
for example because the user hit

42
00:03:02,710 --> 00:03:08,260
the back button, because the activity
terminated itself programmatically, or

43
00:03:08,260 --> 00:03:12,300
even because Android itself has decided
to kill that activity in order to

44
00:03:12,300 --> 00:03:16,800
reap its resources and the activity
is popped off the task back stack.

45
00:03:18,620 --> 00:03:20,440
Let's take a look at
how this process works.

46
00:03:21,920 --> 00:03:26,160
Now this graphic represents an activity
that's running on a device.

47
00:03:26,160 --> 00:03:29,980
And it picks the state of the task
backstack while the application

48
00:03:29,980 --> 00:03:30,490
is running.

49
00:03:31,690 --> 00:03:36,421
The black pointer at the bottom
indicates the current snapshot.

50
00:03:36,421 --> 00:03:42,004
Now in this application, this first
launch, it starts up Activity 1, which

51
00:03:42,004 --> 00:03:47,689
is then pushed on to the top of the task
backstack as the root of the current task.

52
00:03:47,689 --> 00:03:48,866
Now, as you can see,

53
00:03:48,866 --> 00:03:52,930
activity one has a single button,
labeled, start activity two.

54
00:03:52,930 --> 00:03:54,090
So the idea here, is that,

55
00:03:54,090 --> 00:03:57,660
if the user presses this button,
activity two will start.

56
00:03:58,970 --> 00:04:03,180
So let's say, now, that the user
presses the Activity 2 button.

57
00:04:03,180 --> 00:04:07,860
At this point, Activity 1 is suspended and
it's state will be captured so

58
00:04:07,860 --> 00:04:11,410
that it can be restored later
if the user returns back to it.

59
00:04:12,590 --> 00:04:18,870
Next, Activity 2 begins and Activity
2 is pushed on to the task backstack.

60
00:04:18,870 --> 00:04:25,069
Now similar to Activity 1, Activity 2 has
a single button labeled Start Activity 3.

61
00:04:25,069 --> 00:04:28,907
If a user presses this button,
activity 3 will start.

62
00:04:28,907 --> 00:04:32,860
So let's say that the user now
presses the Start Activity 3 button.

63
00:04:33,890 --> 00:04:36,910
At this point activity 2 will be
suspended, activity 3 will begin, and

64
00:04:36,910 --> 00:04:42,980
activity 3 will be pushed
onto the task backstack.

65
00:04:42,980 --> 00:04:46,850
Now let's say that the user has
had enough of Activity 3 and

66
00:04:46,850 --> 00:04:51,050
hits the Back button with the idea
of going back to Activity 2.

67
00:04:51,050 --> 00:04:57,030
At this point, Android kills Activity
3 and pops it off the task backstack.

68
00:04:57,030 --> 00:05:02,850
Now because Activity 2 is currently
at the top of the task backstack,

69
00:05:02,850 --> 00:05:07,910
Android will unsuspend or resume that
activity, restoring its state and

70
00:05:07,910 --> 00:05:10,420
bringing it back in to view on the device.

71
00:05:12,850 --> 00:05:17,400
Now as we saw with the task backstack
examples, Android activities come and

72
00:05:17,400 --> 00:05:20,110
go and come again and go away for good.

73
00:05:20,110 --> 00:05:21,920
They have a life cycle.

74
00:05:24,610 --> 00:05:25,470
And importantly, for

75
00:05:25,470 --> 00:05:30,740
you as a developer, your applications are
not really in control of this lifecycle.

76
00:05:30,740 --> 00:05:35,490
Some lifecycle changes, for example,
depend on choices that the user makes.

77
00:05:35,490 --> 00:05:37,470
Like pressing the Back button or
the Home button.

78
00:05:38,840 --> 00:05:41,960
Other lifecycle changes
depend on Android itself.

79
00:05:41,960 --> 00:05:46,360
For example, if your device is running
low on memory Android can decide to

80
00:05:46,360 --> 00:05:48,950
kill activities that
are currently suspended.

81
00:05:48,950 --> 00:05:52,800
And it will do that knowing that it will
need to recreate them later if the user

82
00:05:52,800 --> 00:05:53,890
navigates back to them.

83
00:05:54,970 --> 00:05:58,270
So let's talk a bit more about
the activity lifecycle and

84
00:05:58,270 --> 00:06:01,710
the particular lifecycle changes
that activities go through.

85
00:06:02,780 --> 00:06:08,910
For example, once an activity is started,
it can be in a resumed or running state.

86
00:06:08,910 --> 00:06:10,050
And while it's in this state,

87
00:06:10,050 --> 00:06:13,000
the activity is visible and
the user can interact with it.

88
00:06:14,170 --> 00:06:17,430
An activity can also be paused,
for instance,

89
00:06:17,430 --> 00:06:20,580
when a new activity starts
to pop up in front of it.

90
00:06:20,580 --> 00:06:24,800
In this situation the activity may
still be partially visible but

91
00:06:24,800 --> 00:06:27,570
the user can't interact with
it because the user will be

92
00:06:27,570 --> 00:06:30,120
interacting with a new
activity that's starting up.

93
00:06:32,230 --> 00:06:33,810
Prior to version 3.0,

94
00:06:33,810 --> 00:06:37,690
Android could terminate activities
once they went in to the paused state.

95
00:06:39,260 --> 00:06:41,100
Now finally, the activity can be stopped.

96
00:06:42,180 --> 00:06:45,590
And when it stopped,
that activity is no longer visible.

97
00:06:45,590 --> 00:06:47,090
And Android is free to terminate.

98
00:06:47,090 --> 00:06:49,500
And this is important to reiterate.

99
00:06:49,500 --> 00:06:52,400
It can terminate stopped activities.

100
00:06:52,400 --> 00:06:56,280
And it does so knowing that it
might need to recreate them later,

101
00:06:56,280 --> 00:06:57,880
if the users navigate back to them.

102
00:06:59,360 --> 00:07:02,480
Now your activities will often
need to behave differently during

103
00:07:02,480 --> 00:07:04,940
different parts of their life cycle.

104
00:07:04,940 --> 00:07:08,170
For instance,
if your activity is showing an animation,

105
00:07:08,170 --> 00:07:14,110
then pops up a partially transparent
dialog style activity in front of it,

106
00:07:14,110 --> 00:07:19,200
well you might want to pause the animation
while the user response to the dialog and

107
00:07:19,200 --> 00:07:23,609
then restart the animation once
that dialog activity finishes.

108
00:07:24,700 --> 00:07:29,780
In order to support scenarios like this,
Android announces lifecycle changes

109
00:07:29,780 --> 00:07:34,180
to activity by calling specific lifecycle,
or template, methods.

110
00:07:35,870 --> 00:07:37,490
And some of these methods are shown here.

111
00:07:38,990 --> 00:07:43,450
Each is named, as you can see,
on something or another, on create when

112
00:07:43,450 --> 00:07:47,990
the activity is about to be created,
on start when the activity is about to

113
00:07:47,990 --> 00:07:53,240
become visible, all the way to onDestroy,
when the activity's about to be destroyed.

114
00:07:53,240 --> 00:07:58,580
And if you want to take some specific
action when your activity changes state,

115
00:07:58,580 --> 00:08:02,440
then you need to override one or
more of these methods in your activity.

116
00:08:03,670 --> 00:08:04,480
So, let's take a look at

117
00:08:04,480 --> 00:08:07,700
how these different methods
interrelate with each other.

118
00:08:07,700 --> 00:08:11,890
Now, this graphic depicts the orders
in which activity lifecycle methods

119
00:08:11,890 --> 00:08:12,470
can be called.

120
00:08:12,470 --> 00:08:15,340
And the important thing to remember here

121
00:08:15,340 --> 00:08:19,420
is that Android applications don't
work completely by themselves.

122
00:08:19,420 --> 00:08:21,280
Instead there's a clear back and

123
00:08:21,280 --> 00:08:25,580
forth collaboration between
your application and Android.

124
00:08:25,580 --> 00:08:29,910
And you have to understand the rules
of this collaboration if you

125
00:08:29,910 --> 00:08:32,480
want your Android applications
to function properly.

126
00:08:33,910 --> 00:08:39,170
So let's imagine a simple application
with one activity that starts up,

127
00:08:40,340 --> 00:08:42,270
waits for a moment, and then exits.

128
00:08:43,530 --> 00:08:47,020
In this simple case when that
application is launched,

129
00:08:47,020 --> 00:08:50,080
Android will call
the activity's onCreate method.

130
00:08:51,440 --> 00:08:54,056
Then Android will call its onStart method.

131
00:08:54,056 --> 00:08:57,470
And then onResume.

132
00:08:57,470 --> 00:09:02,250
After which the activity's user interface
will appear on the device's screen.

133
00:09:02,250 --> 00:09:04,140
And the user can interact with it.

134
00:09:04,140 --> 00:09:07,790
After a minute of so, our example
activity will begin to shut down.

135
00:09:08,790 --> 00:09:12,587
And at this point, Android will call
that activity's onPause method.

136
00:09:13,820 --> 00:09:17,920
Then, onStop and then, finally, onDestroy.

137
00:09:17,920 --> 00:09:20,860
And at this point the activity
is completely dead.

138
00:09:22,120 --> 00:09:26,310
So as you can see the entire lifetime
of the activity runs from the start of

139
00:09:26,310 --> 00:09:28,950
onCreate to the end of onDestroy.

140
00:09:31,430 --> 00:09:36,500
Now when this simple activity started,
it wasn't visible on the screen.

141
00:09:36,500 --> 00:09:39,630
At some point, it became visible,
and at some point later,

142
00:09:39,630 --> 00:09:42,570
it became invisible as it
was removed from the screen.

143
00:09:43,680 --> 00:09:48,160
When activities are about to become
visible, Android calls the on start, and

144
00:09:48,160 --> 00:09:50,290
sometimes, the on restart method.

145
00:09:51,380 --> 00:09:54,290
When activities are about
to become invisible,

146
00:09:54,290 --> 00:09:56,350
Android calls the onStop method.

147
00:09:57,600 --> 00:10:01,090
And so, you can think of the visible
lifetime of an activity as occurring

148
00:10:01,090 --> 00:10:05,570
between the startup calls to onStart and
the end of calls to onStop.

149
00:10:05,570 --> 00:10:11,890
And, finally, while an activity is
visible on the screen, there are times

150
00:10:11,890 --> 00:10:16,180
when the user can interact with it, and
there are times when he or she can't.

151
00:10:16,180 --> 00:10:18,970
And for example, this can happen
when a device goes to sleep.

152
00:10:20,030 --> 00:10:24,430
In that case, the user can't
interact with the activity anymore,

153
00:10:24,430 --> 00:10:27,670
even though it still is
the foreground activity.

154
00:10:27,670 --> 00:10:29,670
So when activities
are about to be ready for

155
00:10:29,670 --> 00:10:32,670
user interaction,
Android calls the onResume method.

156
00:10:33,820 --> 00:10:37,670
When activities are about to stop
being able to interact with the user,

157
00:10:37,670 --> 00:10:40,550
Android calls the onPause method.

158
00:10:40,550 --> 00:10:45,130
And so, you can think of the visible and
in foreground lifetime of an activity

159
00:10:45,130 --> 00:10:50,755
as occurring between the start of calls to
onResume, and the end of calls to onPause.

160
00:10:53,030 --> 00:10:56,360
Let's walk through an example of
this based on the map location

161
00:10:56,360 --> 00:10:59,100
application that we saw
in earlier lessons.

162
00:11:00,890 --> 00:11:06,720
First the user goes to the home screen,
and launches the map location application.

163
00:11:08,840 --> 00:11:11,110
This causes the map location
activity to start up.

164
00:11:12,340 --> 00:11:16,300
At which point Android calls
map locations onCreate method.

165
00:11:18,720 --> 00:11:24,830
onCreate initializes the activity and then
Android continues by calling on start.

166
00:11:26,330 --> 00:11:31,170
And at this point map location is visible
but not yet ready for user interaction.

167
00:11:33,200 --> 00:11:34,830
Android now calls onResume.

168
00:11:37,058 --> 00:11:42,400
After onResume completes an application
will soon be both visible and

169
00:11:42,400 --> 00:11:43,710
ready for user interaction.

170
00:11:43,710 --> 00:11:49,930
And at this point the user will normally
enter an address in the address box.

171
00:11:54,610 --> 00:11:55,920
Once the address has been entered,

172
00:11:56,930 --> 00:12:02,140
the user will normally press the Show Map
button which will launch Google maps.

173
00:12:03,360 --> 00:12:08,840
Now, as Google Maps starts up,
it will have its own initial activity

174
00:12:08,840 --> 00:12:13,400
that will go through its own life cycle,
and receive its own life cycle callbacks.

175
00:12:13,400 --> 00:12:17,680
But let's continue looking at what's
going on with the map location activity.

176
00:12:17,680 --> 00:12:20,610
Now because Google maps is about
to come into the foreground and

177
00:12:20,610 --> 00:12:26,260
cover up map location, the map location
activity will first receive a call to its

178
00:12:26,260 --> 00:12:32,490
on pause method, and soon after this,
map location will no longer be visible.

179
00:12:32,490 --> 00:12:35,580
And Android will call its onStop method.

180
00:12:37,740 --> 00:12:40,020
Now eventually Google Maps
will appear on the screen and

181
00:12:41,500 --> 00:12:42,700
the user can interact with it.

182
00:12:43,740 --> 00:12:47,920
But at some point the user will be done
with Google Maps and might, for example,

183
00:12:47,920 --> 00:12:51,060
choose to return back to map location.

184
00:12:51,060 --> 00:12:53,610
For instance, to map a new address.

185
00:12:53,610 --> 00:12:57,190
So let's assume that the user now hits
the Back button to return back to

186
00:12:57,190 --> 00:12:58,800
map location.

187
00:12:58,800 --> 00:13:02,930
At this point, map location must be
brought back into the foreground.

188
00:13:04,630 --> 00:13:09,180
So as Android does this,
it will first call onRestart and

189
00:13:09,180 --> 00:13:14,310
then onStart, and soon after this
map location will again be visible.

190
00:13:15,560 --> 00:13:18,680
Next, Android will call map
locations onResume method.

191
00:13:20,090 --> 00:13:24,580
Soon after which the map location activity
will be both visible and ready for

192
00:13:24,580 --> 00:13:25,530
user interaction.

193
00:13:27,730 --> 00:13:32,530
And finally when the user eventually
loses interest in map location, he or

194
00:13:32,530 --> 00:13:37,780
she might hit the back button again,
in this case to end the application.

195
00:13:37,780 --> 00:13:38,690
At this point,

196
00:13:38,690 --> 00:13:43,580
Android will again go through the process
of removing map location from the screen.

197
00:13:43,580 --> 00:13:48,917
It will first call onPause,
then onStop, and then this time it

198
00:13:48,917 --> 00:13:55,174
will call onDestroy, before completely
terminating the application.

